    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/8/48320.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "right news roundup information overload our glad you with us eight hundred nine four one chon if you want to be a part of the program so since nineteen fifty four there have been five separate occasions where the house of representatives has changed in terms of the election and change power. You will see this in January when Nancy Pelosi gives up the gavel and Republicans now will be in charge of the House. That's important for all the reasons we've discussed, the power of the purse, the power to subpoena committee assignments. I mean, it's a big win for Republicans, a necessary win, and it's a great check and balance on Democrats. Now, in all five of these occasions or leading up to this one in January, there's never been a moment where the opposition in the Senate would go along with the party in power and pass an honorable spending bill for the entire next year. That's never happened before. And right now we have Republicans, obviously led by Mitch McConnell, but other people like Richard Shelby who's retiring at the end of the year and some other people pushing this omnibus spending bill, which is the entire year spending bill before the new Republican Congress takes power in the House of Representatives in January. And it makes no sense at all whatsoever. Historically, what these lame duck sessions would do, they would pass a continuing resolution to fund the government in the interim. And then the incoming, in this case, incoming Republican majority would then have a say in the budget process for next year. And for whatever reason, Republican leadership in the Senate has decided that they want to work with Schumer and with Biden and go along with a year, a year's worth of spending, taking away, you know, what is really a powerful tool for the new Republican majority in the House and stop some of the insane spending that we have with what a deficit now and a debt of what $31 trillion is insane. I cannot get a reasonable explanation as to why they're doing it. Many Republicans are speaking out against it. Senator Mike Lee has been keeping me in the loop. He's apoplectic about it, as are many other Republican senators. Senator Marsha Blackburn from Tennessee is with us to give us her take on all of this. Senator, how are you?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_09",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [],
    "assumptions": [],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
